{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And he heard, etc.</b> One needs to know for what purpose the verse assigns him the designation ״priest of Midian,״ for this is not a distinction but a demotion, and properly the earlier designations should not have been mentioned." "Or perhaps it intends to praise the man by saying <b>priest of Midian</b>, to say that although he had two qualities alluded to in ״priest of Midian,״ namely that he was a prince of Midian and zealous for idolatry, none the less he did two things contrary and opposite to both particulars. Against his being a great man it says <b>father-in-law of Moses</b>, for he gave his daughter in marriage to a man whose greatness was not yet known — and this is something that men of authority would resent, and all the more so one who is distinguished among his nation, who would resent giving his daughter to a man of no reputation. And against his being zealous for idolatry, as is the plain meaning of the word priest, the verse says at the end of the passage what it says (Exodus 18:11): ״עַתָּה יָדַעְתִּי כִּי גָדוֹל ה׳ מִכָּל הָאֱלֹהִים״. And this matter contains no novelty or great distinction in affirming it in the case of a single gentile to establish it in the Torah; rather, by means of the fact that it prefaced designating him by the name ״priest of Midian,״ it is a great thing that the priest of a nation, one of their own, should testify among them that the falsehood that issues from their mouths is vanity." "<b>Further</b>, a reason for its saying <b>priest of Midian</b>: to say that through this he attained to know all that HaShem had done, for to great men everything is revealed and known in truth without addition or diminution, which is not so for those who are not authorities — even if news becomes known to them, they do not know all the things that were done." "<b>et all that</b>, etc. — this too declares the man's praise, that he exerted himself to know all the details of the events, and this declares that he loved Jacob, for you will see a man who hates his fellow is disgusted by hearing his praises and his good fortune and refrains from knowing his favorable condition — unlike a beloved to his beloved. And HaShem recorded in His Torah that this man loved Israel. Further, it intends by close analysis [to ask] yet another question: why did the verse single out this particular detail of the Exodus of Israel from Egypt from the general category of ״all that He did״? And further, that it is the essence of all that He did and there should have been no need to state it explicitly." "<b>However</b>, since Jethro undoubtedly knew the might of Egypt's imprisonment, as is stated in their words (Mekhilta) that prisoners of Egypt were never released, and all the more so according to their words (Sanhedrin 106a; Exodus Rabbah 1:1) that they said Jethro was one of Pharaoh's advisors and knowledgeable about Egypt and its prisoners — therefore even if he heard ״that He brought out,״ etc., he would not have justified the Exodus in his heart. For this reason the verse prefaced a matter through which it justified ״for He brought out,״ and said <b>And Jethro heard et all that [Elohim] had done</b>, meaning all the wonders and awesome things that HaShem did in justifying the prophet, which is what it means by <b>which He had done for Moses</b>, and the wonders He did to deliver Israel — both in bringing down those who held them, and in distinguishing them from the plagues, and in the miracle of the sea, etc. — and through this he knew <b>that He had brought out</b>, etc. And therefore it did not say ״and that He brought out,״ for without hearing all that was done through the wonders, he would not have justified it even if he heard that He sent them away, for he would not believe; and if he believed, he would not trust it, for he would say they will yet pursue them, and if they pursued and could not, they will prepare another camp and pursue." "<b>However,</b> in that He heard <b>all that</b> etc., the verse justified ״כִּי הוֹצִיא.״ And you will find that our Sages of blessed memory disagreed (Zevaḥim 116a) about which of the reports moved him: some said the splitting of the Sea of Reeds, and some said the war with Amalek — and this fits our words. And according to the one who says the war with Amalek, from the fact that the verse juxtaposes to it ״וַיִּשְׁמַע,״ this tells that what he heard of all the wonders did not suffice for him, for one might attribute the whole matter to Pharaoh's recklessness, that he rebelled against the emissary of HaShem — not for the purpose of saving Israel, for HaShem would not have gone to such trouble for their sake. And if so, Israel has no assurance against the nations that would rise against them. Therefore the verse says that he heard of the war with Amalek, and our Sages of blessed memory already wondered (Mekhilta) at the magnitude of that accursed war. And it appears that according to this approach, the verse's saying ״כִּי הוֹצִיא״ may be explained as follows: in that he heard all that He did, and this includes the war with Amalek, thereby he justified it — for all the signs and wonders were solely for the exaltation and love of Israel to bring them out, and for their sake He did all that He did — and the report of Amalek will demonstrate this." "<b>Further,</b> it intends in saying <b>כִּי הוֹצִיח</b> to attach after it the account of Jethro's taking Zipporah etc., after the manner of their statement of blessed memory (Exodus Rabbah 4:4): for Jethro said to Moses: Why are you taking your wife and so forth? He said to him: Israel is destined to receive the Torah at Mount Sinai, and my children will not be included. He said to him: Go in peace and so forth — end of quote. And therefore the verse says <b>כִּי הוֹצִיא וַיִּקַּח</b> etc., to fulfill his word."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:29:43.036393677Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}